00:00:28.935 --> 00:00:42.417
Everything between order packed and delivered: geofenced serviceability, rider GPS ingestion, the live rider index, dispatch (which rider takes which order), ETAs and live tracking on the customer's screen.

10
00:00:42.697 --> 00:00:48.116
This is the design Uber or Swiggy dispatch interview question for a quick-commerce platform.

11
00:00:48.396 --> 00:01:03.692
Earlier chapters deferred all of it here: polygon serviceability (Ch 01), telemetry ingestion (Ch 03, Ch 14), H3 or geohash indexing and dispatch algorithms (Ch 06).

12
00:01:05.042 --> 00:01:08.263
L1: geo indexes (real Redis for riders).

13
00:01:08.543 --> 00:01:14.367
Serviceability over 400 store polygons and 200,000 customer locations:

14
00:01:14.647 --> 00:01:22.733
Testing every polygon took 1,457 ms (136.7 polygon tests per lookup).

15
00:01:23.013 --> 00:01:29.391
A 500 m grid index took 78 ms (1.9 tests per lookup), with the same answers.

16
00:01:29.671 --> 00:01:43.858
For the nearest 5 of 20,000 riders, brute force took 780 µs p50, a 1 km grid 11 µs, and Redis GEOSEARCH 468 µs including the network round trip.

17
00:01:44.138 --> 00:01:44.949
L2:

18
00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:47.533
GPS ingestion (real Redis).

19
00:01:47.813 --> 00:01:53.039
250,000 pings from 50,000 riders, 3% delivered late:

20
00:01:54.389 --> 00:02:02.539
Two commands per ping reached ~12,800 pings per second and left 480 riders at an older position.

21
00:02:02.819 --> 00:02:06.680
An ordering-safe Lua script per ping gave 0 stale riders.

22
00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.312
Pipelining reached ~94,500 pings per second.

23
00:02:11.592 --> 00:02:19.571
A movement filter for the live index cut Redis writes from 250,000 to 145,061.

24
00:02:19.851 --> 00:02:21.259
L3: dispatch.

25
00:02:21.539 --> 00:02:24.483
6 dark stores times 10 riders at evening peak:

26
00:02:24.763 --> 00:02:30.224
FIFO to riders standing at the store delivered 40% within the 20-minute promise.

27
00:02:31.574 --> 00:02:35.841
Also assigning riders on their way back raised that to 53%.

28
00:02:36.121 --> 00:02:40.387
Batching two orders to nearby customers raised it to 61%.

29
00:02:40.667 --> 00:02:47.793
Both together reached 69%, with the average falling from 24.8 to 18.0 minutes.

30
00:02:48.073 --> 00:02:48.947
L4:

31
00:02:49.227 --> 00:02:49.953
ETAs.

32
00:02:50.233 --> 00:02:54.307
Across 6,677 evaluated deliveries:

33
00:02:54.587 --> 00:03:01.179
A city-wide speed was off by 4.6 min on average, and 49% arrived after the promise.

34
00:03:02.529 --> 00:03:09.335
Zone times hour speeds and learned door times per building brought that to 2.4 min and 28%.

35
00:03:09.615 --> 00:03:16.548
Re-estimating halfway from the rider's actual pace brought it to 1.2 min and 11.5%.

36
