Part 1.

39
00:03:22.365 --> 00:03:24.285
Introduction and Why It Matters.

40
00:03:25.535 --> 00:03:28.969
A story: the rider who was 2 minutes away for 20 minutes.

41
00:03:29.249 --> 00:03:44.993
The first delivery system was simple: customers served by pincode, the nearest idle rider at the store assigned when an order was packed, rider positions written to a table every 4 seconds, and an ETA of distance ÷ 18 km per hour.

42
00:03:45.273 --> 00:03:46.148
At scale:

43
00:03:46.428 --> 00:03:48.647
Serviceability was wrong at the edges.

44
00:03:48.927 --> 00:03:51.721
Pincodes don't match 3 km delivery radii.

45
00:03:52.001 --> 00:04:01.665
Customers across a lake or a highway were accepted and then waited 40 minutes; customers just inside the radius but in the wrong pincode were turned away.

46
00:04:03.015 --> 00:04:04.679
The position table melted.

47
00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:11.253
50,000 riders times 1 ping every 4 s is 12,500 writes per second.

48
00:04:11.533 --> 00:04:17.143
Updates queued; live positions were a minute old, and dispatch sent riders who had already left.

49
00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:19.045
Positions jumped backwards.

50
00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:27.730
Phones buffer pings in tunnels and send them later; a late ping overwrote a newer position, and the customer's map showed the rider reversing.

51
00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:30.229
Riders waited while orders waited.

52
00:04:30.509 --> 00:04:36.589
At 8 pm, orders queued for riders at the store while riders were two minutes away on their way back.

53
00:04:36.869 --> 00:04:39.877
Dispatch only considered riders standing at the store.

54
00:04:41.227 --> 00:04:43.296
The ETA lied most at the worst time.

55
00:04:43.576 --> 00:04:49.891
Arriving in 2 minutes in the evening jam, and in the gated complex whose security desk takes five minutes.

56
00:04:50.171 --> 00:04:53.755
Support calls peaked exactly when the ETA was least honest.

57
00:04:54.035 --> 00:04:58.493
Delivery is where the product's promise (in 20 minutes) is kept or broken.

58
00:04:58.773 --> 00:05:04.576
This chapter designs the geo, streaming, optimisation and estimation systems behind it.

59
00:05:05.926 --> 00:05:06.609
Quote:

60
00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:08.553
The thesis of this chapter.

61
00:05:08.833 --> 00:05:21.078
Delivery is a real-time, geo-indexed control loop: positions stream in, an index answers who is near, an optimiser decides who goes where, and an estimator tells the customer when.

62
00:05:21.358 --> 00:05:25.945
Geo questions are answered by a coarse index plus an exact check (L1).

63
00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:32.902
Telemetry is a high-volume stream that must be order-safe and thinned before it hits the live index (L2).

64
00:05:33.182 --> 00:05:40.670
Dispatch is an optimisation over the next few minutes, predicted availability and batching beat nearest-idle-rider (L3).

65
00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:49.953
ETAs come from learned speeds, learned last-100-metre times and live progress, and promises are sized from the error distribution (L4).

