193
00:11:49.469 --> 00:11:52.776
(near-ties can swap: haversine versus flat distance).

194
00:11:54.126 --> 00:12:02.830
Geohash encodes lat or lon into a string prefix: nearby points usually share prefixes, but neighbours across cell boundaries don't.

195
00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:04.966
Always search neighbouring cells too.

196
00:12:05.246 --> 00:12:11.966
H3 (Uber's hexagonal grid) and S2 (Google's) give uniform cells and hierarchical resolutions.

197
00:12:12.246 --> 00:12:20.097
H3 hexagons have equidistant neighbours, which is useful for zone-level aggregates such as demand, supply and surge.

198
00:12:21.447 --> 00:12:32.775
Serviceability as data: store polygons are edited by ops, versioned, and compiled into a per-city index (cell, then candidate stores) that every service loads in memory.

199
00:12:33.055 --> 00:12:39.369
Dynamic overrides (rain, store overload) shrink polygons or change priorities in real time.

200
00:12:39.649 --> 00:12:41.484
Straight line is only a filter.

201
00:12:41.764 --> 00:12:49.017
Final ranking needs road distance or ETA (L4), especially across rivers, railway lines and one-way systems.

202
00:12:50.367 --> 00:12:51.797
Telemetry ingestion.

203
00:12:52.077 --> 00:13:03.170
Rider apps send positions over HTTP or 2 or MQTT to a gateway, which writes them to Kafka keyed by rider id (per-rider order within a partition).

204
00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:19.194
Consumers then feed: the live index (Redis GEO + last-seen hash) for dispatch and presence (Ch 41). the tracking fan-out for customers watching their order. the history store (time-series or lake) for ETAs, disputes and payouts.

