It runs to 105 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

493
00:36:00.394 --> 00:36:07.221
EtaL4.java, run with: java EtaL4.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

494
00:36:07.501 --> 00:36:09.357
HOW LONG UNTIL THE RIDER ARRIVES?

495
00:36:09.637 --> 00:36:23.887
40,000 synthetic deliveries (fixed seed) over 30 days in a city with 25 zones, each. with its own traffic by hour (evening peaks are slow, some zones much worse), and 2,000 destination buildings whose.

496
00:36:26.737 --> 00:36:27.655
On screen:

497
00:36:27.935 --> 00:36:36.639
40,000 deliveries; learn from days 1 to 25, evaluate 6,677 on days 26 to 30.

498
00:36:36.919 --> 00:36:39.265
Promise =. estimate + 2 min.

499
00:36:39.545 --> 00:36:44.239
ETA method mean error p90 error arrived after the. promised. time.

500
00:36:44.519 --> 00:36:54.524
E1) straight line times 1.4 at 18 km per hour + 2 min 4.6 min 10.5 min 49.0%.

501
00:36:54.804 --> 00:37:01.972
E2) learned speed per (zone, hour) + 2 min 3.2 min 7.4 min 40.0%.

502
00:37:02.252 --> 00:37:09.761
E3) E2 + learned door time per building 2.4 min 5.2 min 27.7%.

503
00:37:10.041 --> 00:37:26.617
E4) E3 + re-estimate halfway from actual progress 1.2 min 2.7 min 11.5%. then a single city-wide speed is wrong in both directions: too pessimistic off-peak. badly optimistic in the evening.

504
00:37:26.897 --> 00:37:28.412
The rest is in the chapter.

505
00:37:31.262 --> 00:37:32.115
Reading it:

506
00:37:32.395 --> 00:37:38.390
One city-wide speed breaks half the promises (49%), mostly in the evening jams.

507
00:37:38.670 --> 00:37:43.769
Zone times hour speeds fix much of the ride error, but a big error remains at the door.

508
00:37:44.049 --> 00:37:50.406
Door time per building brings the mean error to 2.4 minutes and late arrivals to 28%.

509
00:37:51.756 --> 00:37:58.113
Re-estimating halfway from the rider's actual pace cuts late arrivals to 11.5%.

510
00:37:58.393 --> 00:38:03.748
This helps only customers still waiting, and the second half of the ride still has its own noise.

511
00:38:04.028 --> 00:38:10.001
An earlier version of this lab made the second half identical to the first, which made E4 look perfect.

512
00:38:10.281 --> 00:38:11.881
The noise is there on purpose.

513
00:38:13.231 --> 00:38:14.170
Part 6.

514
00:38:14.450 --> 00:38:15.730
Production Concerns.

515
00:38:16.980 --> 00:38:18.132
Failure modes.

516
00:38:18.412 --> 00:38:19.756
Here is a quick table:

517
00:38:20.036 --> 00:38:22.319
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

518
00:38:22.599 --> 00:38:24.156
Brute-force geo queries.

519
00:38:24.436 --> 00:38:25.247
Symptom:

520
00:38:25.527 --> 00:38:28.087
Slow serviceability and dispatch (L1).

521
00:38:28.367 --> 00:38:29.369
Mitigation:

522
00:38:29.649 --> 00:38:33.041
Grid or H3 or geohash index + exact check.

523
00:38:33.321 --> 00:38:34.601
Per-ping round trips.

524
00:38:34.881 --> 00:38:35.692
Symptom:

525
00:38:35.972 --> 00:38:38.191
Stale live index at peak (L2).

526
00:38:38.471 --> 00:38:39.473
Mitigation:

527
00:38:39.753 --> 00:38:41.204
Batching, pipelining.

528
00:38:41.484 --> 00:38:42.508
Late pings.

529
00:38:42.788 --> 00:38:43.599
Symptom:

530
00:38:43.879 --> 00:38:45.863
Riders move backwards (L2).

531
00:38:46.143 --> 00:38:47.145
Mitigation:

532
00:38:47.425 --> 00:38:49.409
Timestamp-guarded atomic updates.

533
00:38:49.689 --> 00:38:51.119
Every ping written live.

534
00:38:51.399 --> 00:38:52.209
Symptom:

535
00:38:52.489 --> 00:38:54.388
Write amplification (L2).

536
00:38:54.668 --> 00:38:55.671
Mitigation:

537
00:38:55.951 --> 00:38:58.191
Movement thinning; adaptive ping rates.

538
00:38:58.471 --> 00:39:00.263
Nearest-idle-rider dispatch.

539
00:39:00.543 --> 00:39:01.353
Symptom:

540
00:39:01.633 --> 00:39:04.385
Orders wait while riders return (L3).

541
00:39:04.665 --> 00:39:05.668
Mitigation:

542
00:39:05.948 --> 00:39:08.401
Predicted availability, batching, matching.

543
00:39:08.681 --> 00:39:09.684
Naive ETAs.

544
00:39:09.964 --> 00:39:10.775
Symptom:

545
00:39:11.055 --> 00:39:13.273
Broken promises at peak (L4).

546
00:39:13.553 --> 00:39:14.556
Mitigation:

547
00:39:14.836 --> 00:39:17.993
Zone times hour speeds, door times, live pace.

548
00:39:19.343 --> 00:39:20.581
Frozen tracking.

549
00:39:20.861 --> 00:39:21.671
Symptom:

550
00:39:21.951 --> 00:39:23.914
Anxious customers, support calls.

551
00:39:24.194 --> 00:39:25.197
Mitigation:

552
00:39:25.477 --> 00:39:27.525
Last updated, extrapolation.

553
00:39:27.805 --> 00:39:29.810
Serviceability edits break zones.

554
00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:30.901
Symptom:

555
00:39:31.181 --> 00:39:32.567
Unserviceable areas.

556
00:39:32.847 --> 00:39:33.850
Mitigation:

557
00:39:34.130 --> 00:39:37.330
Versioned polygons, validation, instant rollback.

558
00:39:38.680 --> 00:39:39.768
Observability.

559
00:39:40.048 --> 00:39:47.173
Live index freshness (p99 age of positions), ingestion lag per partition, dropped late pings.

560
00:39:47.453 --> 00:39:53.213
Dispatch: time from packed to assigned, offer decline rate, batch rate, promise-hit rate per store.

561
00:39:53.493 --> 00:39:58.507
ETA error distributions by zone, hour and building type; promise calibration.

562
00:39:58.787 --> 00:40:00.771
Tracking stream counts and staleness.

563
00:40:02.121 --> 00:40:03.678
Security and privacy.

564
00:40:03.958 --> 00:40:13.387
Location is sensitive personal data for riders and customers: purpose limitation, retention limits, access controls (Ch 36).

565
00:40:13.667 --> 00:40:19.811
Show rider locations only during active deliveries; mask customer addresses until assignment.

566
00:40:20.091 --> 00:40:27.643
Detect GPS spoofing (fake-location apps) with consistency checks: speed, accelerometer, network location.

567
00:40:28.993 --> 00:40:29.889
Performance.

568
00:40:30.169 --> 00:40:36.953
In-memory geo indexes in every service that needs them; Redis GEO for shared, frequently updated data.

569
00:40:37.233 --> 00:40:43.057
Dispatch per store cluster (partitioned by geography) so optimisation problems stay small.

570
00:40:43.337 --> 00:40:49.076
ETA computation cached per (zone, hour) with request-time door and progress adjustments.

571
00:40:50.426 --> 00:40:51.151
Cost.

572
00:40:51.431 --> 00:40:59.367
Rider time is the dominant cost: batching and predicted availability are worth more than any infrastructure saving (L3).

573
00:40:59.647 --> 00:41:04.831
Ping rates cost riders' battery and data as well as our ingestion; adapt them to state.

574
00:41:05.111 --> 00:41:13.005
Maps and routing API calls: cache, and use them only for final ranking of a few candidates (Ch 35).

575
00:41:14.355 --> 00:41:15.293
Part 7.

576
00:41:15.573 --> 00:41:17.280
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

577
00:41:18.530 --> 00:41:19.447
Watch out:

578
00:41:19.727 --> 00:41:20.367
1.

579
00:41:20.647 --> 00:41:22.397
Pincodes as delivery zones.

580
00:41:22.677 --> 00:41:24.405
They don't match delivery radii.

581
00:41:25.755 --> 00:41:26.672
Watch out:

582
00:41:26.952 --> 00:41:27.656
2.

583
00:41:27.936 --> 00:41:29.515
Testing every polygon.

584
00:41:29.795 --> 00:41:34.552
136 polygon tests per lookup instead of ~2 (L1).

585
00:41:35.902 --> 00:41:36.819
Watch out:

586
00:41:37.099 --> 00:41:37.846
3.

587
00:41:38.126 --> 00:41:39.641
Geohash without neighbours.

588
00:41:39.921 --> 00:41:42.779
Points near cell edges miss their nearest riders.

589
00:41:44.129 --> 00:41:45.047
Watch out:

590
00:41:45.327 --> 00:41:46.031
4.

591
00:41:46.311 --> 00:41:49.489
One database row per rider updated every ping.

592
00:41:49.769 --> 00:41:53.204
Write storms at peak; use a stream + batched live index.

593
00:41:54.554 --> 00:41:55.471
Watch out:

594
00:41:55.751 --> 00:41:56.519
5.

595
00:41:56.799 --> 00:41:58.357
Last-write-wins positions.

596
00:41:58.637 --> 00:42:01.261
Late pings move riders backwards (L2:

597
00:42:01.541 --> 00:42:03.098
480 riders).

598
00:42:04.448 --> 00:42:05.365
Watch out:

599
00:42:05.645 --> 00:42:06.392
6.

600
00:42:06.672 --> 00:42:08.891
Writing every ping to the live index.

601
00:42:09.171 --> 00:42:11.261
Most pings carry no news (L2).

602
00:42:12.611 --> 00:42:13.529
Watch out:

603
00:42:13.809 --> 00:42:14.555
7.

604
00:42:14.835 --> 00:42:17.374
Only idle riders at the store are available.

605
00:42:17.654 --> 00:42:20.875
Orders wait for riders who are two minutes away (L3).

606
00:42:22.225 --> 00:42:23.143
Watch out:

607
00:42:23.423 --> 00:42:24.105
8.

608
00:42:24.385 --> 00:42:25.388
No batching.

609
00:42:25.668 --> 00:42:27.823
Every order costs a full return trip.

610
00:42:29.173 --> 00:42:30.090
Watch out:

611
00:42:30.370 --> 00:42:31.117
9.

612
00:42:31.397 --> 00:42:32.570
Straight-line ETAs.

613
00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:35.773
49% of promises broken (L4).

614
00:42:37.123 --> 00:42:38.040
Watch out:

615
00:42:38.320 --> 00:42:39.024
10.

616
00:42:39.304 --> 00:42:41.395
Ignoring the last 100 metres.

617
00:42:41.675 --> 00:42:44.384
Gated complexes add minutes no map shows.

618
00:42:45.734 --> 00:42:46.651
Watch out:

619
00:42:46.931 --> 00:42:47.763
11.

620
00:42:48.043 --> 00:42:49.174
Promising the mean.

621
00:42:49.454 --> 00:42:51.886
Half the customers are late by construction.

622
00:42:53.236 --> 00:42:54.153
Watch out:

623
00:42:54.433 --> 00:42:55.223
12.

624
00:42:55.503 --> 00:42:58.169
Showing rider location outside deliveries.

625
00:42:58.449 --> 00:43:00.348
Privacy breach and safety risk.

626
00:43:01.698 --> 00:43:02.573
Part 8.

627
00:43:02.853 --> 00:43:04.197
Interview Q and A.

628
00:43:05.447 --> 00:43:06.279
Beginner.

629
00:43:06.559 --> 00:43:07.583
Question 1.

630
00:43:07.863 --> 00:43:11.489
Design the delivery tracking and dispatch system, main components?

631
00:43:11.769 --> 00:43:13.135
Thirty-second answer:

632
00:43:13.415 --> 00:43:36.796
Rider apps stream GPS to a gateway into Kafka; consumers maintain a live geo index (Redis GEO), feed tracking (SSE to customers) and history; a serviceability index answers which store serves an address; dispatch assigns orders to riders using the live index and an ETA service; the ETA service combines learned speeds, door times and live progress.

633
00:43:38.146 --> 00:43:39.149
Question 2.

634
00:43:39.429 --> 00:43:42.117
How do you find the nearest available riders quickly?

635
00:43:42.397 --> 00:43:43.762
Thirty-second answer:

636
00:43:44.042 --> 00:43:45.301
A spatial index:

637
00:43:45.581 --> 00:43:59.085
Redis GEO (geohash in a sorted set) with GEOSEARCH by radius, or an in-memory grid or H3 index with expanding rings; filter by availability; then rank the few candidates by road ETA.

638
00:43:59.365 --> 00:44:01.989
Keep positions fresh and prune stale riders.

639
00:44:03.339 --> 00:44:04.384
Question 3.

640
00:44:04.664 --> 00:44:07.395
How would you decide if an address is serviceable?

641
00:44:07.675 --> 00:44:09.040
Thirty-second answer:

642
00:44:09.320 --> 00:44:25.107
Store delivery zones as polygons; compile them into a grid or H3 index mapping cells to candidate polygons; at lookup, find the cell and run point-in-polygon on the few candidates; apply dynamic overrides (weather, store load).

643
00:44:25.387 --> 00:44:27.733
Keep polygons versioned and validated.

644
00:44:29.083 --> 00:44:30.150
Question 4.

645
00:44:30.430 --> 00:44:33.587
How many writes per second does rider tracking generate?

646
00:44:33.867 --> 00:44:35.233
Thirty-second answer:

647
00:44:35.513 --> 00:44:37.113
Riders times ping rate:

648
00:44:37.393 --> 00:44:44.113
50,000 riders at one ping per 4 s is 12,500 pings per second, with peaks higher.

649
00:44:44.393 --> 00:44:51.091
Each ping goes to Kafka (replicated) and, if newsworthy, to the live index; history is written in batches.

650
00:44:52.441 --> 00:44:53.444
Intermediate.

651
00:44:53.724 --> 00:44:54.833
Question 5.

652
00:44:55.113 --> 00:44:57.887
How do you ingest GPS pings reliably?

653
00:44:58.167 --> 00:44:59.532
Thirty-second answer:

654
00:44:59.812 --> 00:45:18.073
Gateway, then Kafka keyed by rider id; consumers batch updates to the live index with an atomic, timestamp-guarded write (Lua) so late pings don't move riders backwards; thin updates by movement; write raw pings to history; handle GPS noise (snapping, jump filtering).

655
00:45:19.423 --> 00:45:20.533
Question 6.

656
00:45:20.813 --> 00:45:22.925
How does live tracking reach the customer?

657
00:45:23.205 --> 00:45:24.570
Thirty-second answer:

658
00:45:24.850 --> 00:45:41.725
The tracking service consumes location events for active deliveries and pushes smoothed positions and ETAs over SSE to subscribed customers every few seconds, with resume and heartbeats; stale positions show last updated and are extrapolated along the route.

659
00:45:43.075 --> 00:45:44.163
Question 7.

660
00:45:44.443 --> 00:45:47.728
What's the difference between geohash, H3 and S2?

661
00:45:48.008 --> 00:45:49.373
Thirty-second answer:

662
00:45:49.653 --> 00:45:52.064
All map positions to hierarchical cells.

663
00:45:52.344 --> 00:45:59.661
Geohash uses rectangular cells encoded as string prefixes (simple, but uneven sizes and edge effects).

664
00:45:59.941 --> 00:46:06.704
H3 uses hexagons with equidistant neighbours and fixed resolutions (good for aggregates and smoothing).

665
00:46:06.984 --> 00:46:12.637
S2 uses cells on a cube projection with a space-filling curve (good for region coverings).

666
00:46:13.987 --> 00:46:15.011
Question 8.

667
00:46:15.291 --> 00:46:16.891
How do you estimate an ETA?

668
00:46:17.171 --> 00:46:18.537
Thirty-second answer:

669
00:46:18.817 --> 00:46:35.990
Ride time from road distance and learned speeds per zone and hour (or a routing engine with traffic), plus learned last-100-metre time per building, plus packing time for pre-dispatch ETAs; update live from the rider's actual pace; promise at a percentile, not the mean.

670
00:46:37.340 --> 00:46:38.407
Question 9.

671
00:46:38.687 --> 00:46:40.415
How do you manage rider state?

672
00:46:40.695 --> 00:46:42.060
Thirty-second answer:

673
00:46:42.340 --> 00:46:59.791
An explicit state machine (offline, idle at store, assigned, delivering, returning) driven by app actions and geofence events, stored in Redis with the live position, consumed by dispatch; presence from the location stream with grace periods; stuck-state detection.

674
00:47:01.141 --> 00:47:01.909
Senior.

675
00:47:02.189 --> 00:47:03.234
Question 10.

676
00:47:03.514 --> 00:47:05.306
Design the dispatch algorithm.

677
00:47:05.586 --> 00:47:06.951
Thirty-second answer:

678
00:47:07.231 --> 00:47:28.095
Every few seconds per store cluster, solve an assignment problem between orders (packed and soon to be packed) and riders (at store and returning soon), with costs from predicted delivery times, promise-breach penalties and batch detours; allow batches of 2 to 3 compatible orders; send offers with timeouts; re-plan continuously.

679
00:47:29.445 --> 00:47:30.576
Question 11.

680
00:47:30.856 --> 00:47:33.224
How do you keep promises accurate at checkout?

681
00:47:33.504 --> 00:47:34.869
Thirty-second answer:

682
00:47:35.149 --> 00:47:52.899
Compute the promise from the same models dispatch uses: packing time (store load), rider availability (predicted), ride and door ETAs, at a high percentile; adjust serviceability and promises dynamically when stores are overloaded or weather is bad; measure calibration.

683
00:47:54.249 --> 00:47:55.337
Question 12.

684
00:47:55.617 --> 00:47:58.369
How do you handle GPS noise and spoofing?

685
00:47:58.649 --> 00:48:00.014
Thirty-second answer:

686
00:48:00.294 --> 00:48:17.467
Filter impossible jumps (speed thresholds), snap to roads with map matching, smooth for display, fuse with network location; detect spoofing through consistency (speed, sensor data, mock-location flags, repeated identical coordinates) and flag accounts.

687
00:48:18.817 --> 00:48:20.097
Question 13.

688
00:48:20.377 --> 00:48:23.215
How do you partition dispatch as the platform grows?

689
00:48:23.495 --> 00:48:24.860
Thirty-second answer:

690
00:48:25.140 --> 00:48:40.585
By geography: each dispatcher owns a store cluster (a set of H3 cells), keeping optimisation problems small and data local; riders can move between clusters via handoff; cross-cluster rebalancing runs at a slower cadence.

691
00:48:41.935 --> 00:48:43.130
Question 14.

692
00:48:43.410 --> 00:48:47.485
How do you reduce the write load of rider tracking without losing freshness?

693
00:48:47.765 --> 00:48:49.130
Thirty-second answer:

694
00:48:49.410 --> 00:49:03.085
Adaptive ping rates by state (fast while delivering, slow while idle), movement-based thinning for the live index, batching and pipelining, compact encodings, and keeping every raw ping only in the stream and history.

695
00:49:04.435 --> 00:49:05.331
Architect.

696
00:49:05.611 --> 00:49:06.805
Question 15.

697
00:49:07.085 --> 00:49:11.693
Design delivery for a 300-store quick-commerce platform end to end.

698
00:49:11.973 --> 00:49:13.339
Thirty-second answer:

699
00:49:13.619 --> 00:49:29.789
Polygon serviceability compiled to cell indexes; telemetry via Kafka to a timestamp-safe, batched, thinned Redis live index plus history; dispatch per store cluster with predicted availability, batching and matching; ETA service with learned speeds,

700
00:49:30.069 --> 00:49:39.776
door times and live pace; SSE tracking with privacy; models trained daily from history; SLOs on freshness, promise-hit rate and ETA error.

701
00:49:41.126 --> 00:49:42.321
Question 16.

702
00:49:42.601 --> 00:49:45.481
How do you evaluate a new dispatch algorithm safely?

703
00:49:45.761 --> 00:49:47.126
Thirty-second answer:

704
00:49:47.406 --> 00:50:08.227
Replay historical days in a simulator (like L3, with real order and rider traces), compare delivery times, promise hits, rider earnings and fairness; then switchback experiments (alternate algorithms by time slot per store, since stores interact within a cluster) rather than user-level A or B tests; monitor guardrails.

705
00:50:09.577 --> 00:50:10.921
Question 17.

706
00:50:11.201 --> 00:50:14.849
What are the privacy and safety obligations around location?

707
00:50:15.129 --> 00:50:16.495
Thirty-second answer:

708
00:50:16.775 --> 00:50:35.527
Location is personal data for riders and customers: collect for stated purposes (dispatch, tracking, safety, payouts), minimise (show only during active deliveries, approximate before pickup), retain briefly, restrict access, audit, and support safety features (SOS, trip sharing).

709
00:50:36.877 --> 00:50:38.050
Question 18.

710
00:50:38.330 --> 00:50:40.485
Build or buy maps, routing and ETA?

711
00:50:40.765 --> 00:50:42.130
Thirty-second answer:

712
00:50:42.410 --> 00:50:59.093
Buy base maps and routing (maps APIs) or run open-source routing (OSRM, Valhalla, GraphHopper) on OpenStreetMap; build the parts that encode your operations: speeds learned from your riders, door times per building, packing times, and the promise logic.

713
00:51:00.443 --> 00:51:01.680
Question 19.

714
00:51:01.960 --> 00:51:04.840
How does delivery connect to the rest of the platform?

715
00:51:05.120 --> 00:51:06.485
Thirty-second answer:

716
00:51:06.765 --> 00:51:23.320
Checkout uses serviceability and promise ETAs (Ch 46); order events drive dispatch; chat uses rider presence (Ch 41); notifications announce states (Ch 40); payouts use history; support uses tracks and states.

717
00:51:24.670 --> 00:51:25.779
Question 20.

718
00:51:26.059 --> 00:51:28.043
What metrics show delivery is healthy?

719
00:51:28.323 --> 00:51:29.689
Thirty-second answer:

720
00:51:29.969 --> 00:51:46.267
Promise-hit rate by store and hour; order-to-door time distribution; live position freshness; dispatch latency and batch rate; ETA error and calibration; rider utilisation and earnings; offer decline rate; customer where is my order contacts.

